; Dungeons: a named group of levels reached through a world-map location.
;
; The tier that did not exist before the world map (docs/world-map.md). Until
; now a "dungeon" was only an accident of which level stems' stairs happened to
; point at each other, and project.ini's flat `levels` list was the whole
; hierarchy. That list STAYS — it is the editor's universe — and a dungeon
; simply names which of those levels belong to it.
;
; A DUNGEON IS NOT A PLACE. The world-map LOCATION is the place; this is the
; content behind it. That is what lets a found map reveal a location without
; touching the dungeon, and what lets TWO locations share one dungeon — a front
; gate and a back way, each landing somewhere different inside.
;
; A DUNGEON HAS NO START OF ITS OWN (Michael, 2026-09-09). It is a named group
; of levels and nothing more: every way IN carries its own destination — a
; world-map location says which level and cell it opens onto, and the game's
; opening says the same in project.ini. So there is no `entry` field to drift
; out of step with the doors that actually lead here.
;
; Fields:
;   levels   space-separated level stems, in depth order. Every stem must exist
;            in project.ini.
;   tags     flavour, for later content matching (Catalog.h tags).

[crypt]
display = The Crypt
; The starter dungeon: two small floors, a gate at the top and a back way out
; of the lower one.
levels = crypt1 crypt2
tags = stone undead

; NOT CONTENT. The harness's ground is a dungeon so that the checker has a
; coherent world to check — a level belonging to nothing raises an orphan
; warning, and a permanently-warning baseline is one nobody reads.
[eval]
display = The Proving Ground
levels = eval_arena
tags = stone
